	<annotations>
		<arrow id="0" start="(614.2857142857144, 11.714285714285687)" end="(691.142857142857, 94.28571428571426)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="1" type="text/plain" rect="(455.4285714285712, -131.2857142857143, 150.0, 166.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">In this visiualization we choose dropout as our target and we distribute them by scholarships and department</text>
		<arrow id="2" start="(857.7142857142853, 106.28571428571428)" end="(771.7142857142858, 245.42857142857144)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="3" type="text/plain" rect="(827.1428571428573, -91.71428571428572, 418.5714285714282, 166.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">here we will see the relationship of GPA and attendance rate to see the reason of students dropout.

Based on the scatter plot we will see that many of the students have lower GPA even though they have high attendance rate, Because of this we can say that GPA is connected to dropouts.</text>
		<text id="4" type="text/plain" rect="(1335.428571428571, 758.2857142857142, 257.14285714285734, 144.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">In this scatter plot we can see that many students have high stress index due to having low GPA for that reason we can say that having high stress index is connected to students being dropout</text>
		<text id="5" type="text/plain" rect="(272.28571428571433, 853.4285714285716, 291.4285714285714, 166.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">In this one, we can see that many students have low GPA even though they have long study hours, because of this we can say that even if the students have long study hours if they cant understand the lesson they have a high change of dropping out.</text>
		<text id="6" type="text/plain" rect="(62.0, 58.0, 241.42857142857144, 82.85714285714283)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">here we edited the text output of dropout to avoid confussion from 0 to No and 1 to Yes</text>
		<arrow id="7" start="(236.57142857142856, 163.71428571428567)" end="(284.8571428571429, 245.71428571428567)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="8" type="text/plain" rect="(245.42857142857144, 576.2857142857142, 160.0, 100.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">we used this widget to fill the missing value</text>
		<arrow id="9" start="(409.7142857142857, 559.4285714285714)" end="(492.57142857142856, 490.57142857142844)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="10" type="text/plain" rect="(415.7142857142858, 1118.5714285714287, 694.2857142857143, 276.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">Conclusion:
The analysis reveals that academic performance (GPA) is the primary driver of students attrition. While factors like attendance and long study hours are typically associated with success, the data shows that theses do not gurantee a high GPA; students often struggle despite high engagement, leading to increased stress levels. 

Overall, the strongest predictor of dropout status is a low GPA coupled with high stress, suggesting that student retention efforts should focus less on mere participation and more on targeted academic support and mental health intervention to ensure students are efficiently comprehending the material. </text>
		<arrow id="11" start="(1328.0, 768.0)" end="(1236.0, 678.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<arrow id="12" start="(620.0, 882.0)" end="(710.0, 810.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
	</annotations>
